=/            V P O R T   W I R E   P R O T O C O L         \=
==============================================================

                       Version 0 (draft)


--[ 1. SCOPE ]------------------------------------------------

  This document is the specification of the VPort protocol.
  It does not depend on any implementation. A client library
  or a server is compatible with VPort when it follows this
  document byte for byte. libvport and vportd, found in the
  same source tree, are the reference client library and the
  reference server; they are not the protocol.


--[ 2. OVERVIEW ]---------------------------------------------

  A client talks to a server (for example vportd) over a Unix
  domain socket (SOCK_STREAM) or, across the network, over
  TLS (section 8). Messages flow as a continuous byte stream;
  each message is one frame made of an 8 byte header followed
  by a payload. The size of the payload is fixed by the
  opcode, or limited to a range by it.

  All integers are little-endian. There is no padding.


--[ 3. HEADER ]-----------------------------------------------

   0               2               4                       8
   +---------------+---------------+-----------------------+
   |    opcode     |     flags     |      payload_len      |
   |      u16      |      u16      |          u32          |
   +---------------+---------------+-----------------------+

  opcode       identifies the message, see section 5.
  flags        reserved, must be 0.
  payload_len  size of the payload in bytes. It must be a size
               the opcode allows (section 5) and must never
               exceed 1 MiB (1048576).


--[ 4. SESSION ]----------------------------------------------

  1. The client connects to the server. On the Unix socket
     this is a plain connection; on the network it is a TLS
     connection (section 8).
  2. The client sends Hello with the version it speaks.
  3. The server answers Hello with the version it accepts.
  4. The client may then create portals and images, and draw.

  Any message sent before Hello is a protocol violation,
  except ProxyIdentity on a gateway connection (section 8).
  A second Hello is a protocol violation. A version mismatch
  closes the connection.

  The server reads the credentials of the peer (pid, uid, gid)
  from the kernel when a connection is accepted on a Unix
  socket. These are never taken from the protocol.


--[ 5. MESSAGES ]---------------------------------------------

  Opcode  Name             Direction          Payload size
  ------  ---------------  -----------------  ------------
  0x0001  Hello            both                4
  0x0002  ProxyIdentity    client -> server    1 to 128
  0x0010  InitPortal       client -> server   12
  0x0011  ClosePortal      client -> server    4
  0x0020  DrawRect         client -> server   16
  0x0021  DrawImage        client -> server   12
  0x0022  ShowText         client -> server   15 to 270
  0x0030  CreateImage      client -> server    9
  0x0031  ImageData        client -> server    9 to 1048576
  0x0032  DestroyImage     client -> server    4
  0x0040  Commit           client -> server    4
  0x0050  GrabKeyboard     client -> server    4
  0x0051  ReleaseKeyboard  client -> server    4
  0x8001  KeyEvent         server -> client   10
  0x8002  PointerButton    server -> client   10
  0x8003  FocusChanged     server -> client    5
  0x8004  GrabStatus       server -> client    5

  Opcodes from 0x8000 up are sent only by the server.


  Hello (0x0001)

      +---------------+---------------+
      |    version    | capabilities  |
      |      u16      |      u16      |
      +---------------+---------------+

      version       protocol version, currently 1.
      capabilities  reserved, must be 0.


  ProxyIdentity (0x0002)

      +-------------------------------+
      | identity, 1 to 128 bytes      |
      +-------------------------------+

      Sent only by a gateway, as the first message on its
      connection and before Hello. identity is printable ASCII
      without spaces: the Common Name of the client
      certificate. See section 8.


  InitPortal (0x0010)

      +-------------------------------+
      |           portal_id           |
      |              u32              |
      +---------------+---------------+
      |       x       |       y       |
      |      i16      |      i16      |
      +---------------+---------------+
      |       w       |       h       |
      |      u16      |      u16      |
      +---------------+---------------+

      Creates a portal owned by the calling client. The id is
      chosen by the client and must be unused.


  ClosePortal (0x0011)

      +-------------------------------+
      |           portal_id           |
      |              u32              |
      +-------------------------------+

      Destroys a portal and everything drawn in it.


  DrawRect (0x0020)

      +-------------------------------+
      |           portal_id           |
      |              u32              |
      +---------------+---------------+
      |       x       |       y       |
      |      i16      |      i16      |
      +---------------+---------------+
      |       w       |       h       |
      |      u16      |      u16      |
      +---------------+---------------+
      |             rgba              |
      |              u32              |
      +-------------------------------+

      Queues a filled rectangle, relative to the portal.
      Colour is 0xRRGGBBAA. Nothing is shown until Commit.


  DrawImage (0x0021)

      +-------------------------------+
      |           portal_id           |
      |              u32              |
      +-------------------------------+
      |           image_id            |
      |              u32              |
      +---------------+---------------+
      |       x       |       y       |
      |      i16      |      i16      |
      +---------------+---------------+

      Queues the whole image at 1:1, relative to the portal,
      alpha blended. The image must exist when the command is
      sent. Nothing is shown until Commit.


  ShowText (0x0022)

      Offset  Size  Field
      ------  ----  ------------------------------------
      0       4     portal_id  u32
      4       2     x          i16
      6       2     y          i16
      8       2     height     u16
      10      4     rgba       u32
      14      n     text       1 to 256 bytes of UTF-8

      Queues one line of text. x and y are the top left corner
      of the line, relative to the portal. height is the font
      size in pixels, from 1 to 512. Colour is 0xRRGGBBAA.
      The text is not wrapped, and control characters are
      ignored. Nothing is shown until Commit.

      The font is chosen by the server, so the width of the
      text can differ between servers.


  CreateImage (0x0030)

      +-------------------------------+
      |           image_id            |
      |              u32              |
      +---------------+---------------+
      |     width     |    height     |
      |      u16      |      u16      |
      +-------+-------+---------------+
      |format |
      |  u8   |
      +-------+

      Creates an image owned by the client, filled with
      transparent black. Format 0 is RGBA8: four bytes per
      pixel in the order R, G, B, A, straight (not
      premultiplied) alpha, rows from top to bottom, no
      padding. Width and height are 1 to 4096.


  ImageData (0x0031)

      +-------------------------------+
      |           image_id            |
      |              u32              |
      +-------------------------------+
      |            offset             |
      |              u32              |
      +-------------------------------+
      | pixel bytes, at least 1       |
      +-------------------------------+

      Writes bytes into the image starting at the byte offset.
      Several messages can fill one image, and the same bytes
      can be rewritten at any time. The pixels shown are the
      ones in the image when the portal is next composed, so
      streaming an image is: ImageData, then Commit.


  DestroyImage (0x0032)

      +-------------------------------+
      |           image_id            |
      |              u32              |
      +-------------------------------+

      Frees the image. Commands that still refer to it draw
      nothing.


  Commit (0x0040)

      +-------------------------------+
      |           portal_id           |
      |              u32              |
      +-------------------------------+

      Atomically replaces the visible frame of the portal with
      all commands queued since the previous Commit.


  GrabKeyboard (0x0050) and ReleaseKeyboard (0x0051)

      +-------------------------------+
      |           portal_id           |
      |              u32              |
      +-------------------------------+

      See section 7.


  KeyEvent (0x8001)

      +-------------------------------+
      |           portal_id           |
      |              u32              |
      +---------------+---------------+
      |   scancode    |   modifiers   |
      |      u16      |      u16      |
      +-------+-------+---------------+
      |pressed| repeat|
      |  u8   |  u8   |
      +-------+-------+

      scancode is a USB HID usage id (usage page 0x07), so it
      does not depend on the keyboard layout. Modifier bits:
      0x0001 shift, 0x0002 ctrl, 0x0004 alt.


  PointerButton (0x8002)

      +-------------------------------+
      |           portal_id           |
      |              u32              |
      +---------------+---------------+
      |       x       |       y       |
      |      i16      |      i16      |
      +-------+-------+---------------+
      |button |pressed|
      |  u8   |  u8   |
      +-------+-------+

      x and y are relative to the portal. Buttons: 1 left,
      2 middle, 3 right, 4 and 5 extra.


  FocusChanged (0x8003)

      +-------------------------------+-------+
      |           portal_id           |focused|
      |              u32              |  u8   |
      +-------------------------------+-------+


  GrabStatus (0x8004)

      +-------------------------------+-------+
      |           portal_id           | status|
      |              u32              |  u8   |
      +-------------------------------+-------+

      status: 0 denied, 1 granted, 2 released.


--[ 6. COMPOSITION ]------------------------------------------

  Portals are drawn in the order their clients connected and,
  inside one client, by ascending portal_id. A portal drawn
  later covers one drawn earlier.

  Inside a portal, the commands of the last Commit (DrawRect,
  DrawImage and ShowText) are drawn in the order they were
  sent. Every command is clipped to the bounds of its portal
  and to the screen.

  Images belong to the client that created them. A client can
  not refer to the image of another client.


--[ 7. INPUT, FOCUS AND KEYBOARD GRAB ]-----------------------

  Focus. At most one portal has keyboard focus. A new portal
  takes focus unless a keyboard grab is held. A pointer button
  press inside a portal gives it focus, unless a grab is held.
  When the focused portal goes away, the topmost remaining
  portal takes focus. The old and the new owner are told with
  FocusChanged. A client that is denied the grab still gets
  the keyboard events of a portal that has focus.

  Pointer events. PointerButton goes to the topmost portal
  under the pointer. A keyboard grab does not affect it.

  Keyboard grab. A client asks for exclusive keyboard input
  with GrabKeyboard and the server answers with GrabStatus.
  The rules are strict:

    - The identity of the client (section 8) must be in the
      grab policy of the server. A local client is checked by
      uid, a network client by identity. By default the
      policy lets in uid 0 and the uid the server runs as,
      and no network identity at all. The reference server
      reads the lists from VPORT_GRAB_UIDS and
      VPORT_GRAB_IDENTITIES.
    - Only one grab exists at a time. A second request is
      denied; a grab is never taken away from its holder.

  While a grab is held, all keyboard events go to the portal
  of the holder, which also gets focus. The grab ends with
  ReleaseKeyboard, when the portal is closed, or when the
  client disconnects.

  A client that sends a server-only message (opcode 0x8000 or
  higher) is disconnected.


--[ 8. TRANSPORTS AND IDENTITY ]------------------------------

  The words MUST, MUST NOT, SHOULD and MAY in this section
  are used as in RFC 2119.

  A server MUST know who each client is. The identity of a
  client comes from the transport, never from the client.

  Local transport. The Unix domain socket. The identity is
  the uid of the peer, read from the kernel with SO_PEERCRED.

  Network transport. TCP carrying TLS, and inside it the
  protocol exactly as on the Unix socket.

    - A network transport MUST use TLS 1.3 or newer. There
      is no plaintext network transport and none may be
      defined.
    - Both sides MUST authenticate with certificates. A
      server MUST refuse a client without a valid
      certificate, and a client MUST refuse a server without
      a valid one. The certificates MUST be issued by a
      certificate authority that the operator controls.
    - The identity of a network client is the Common Name of
      its certificate.
    - Early data (0-RTT) MUST NOT be accepted.
    - A server MUST NOT read any VPort frame before the TLS
      handshake has finished and the client has been
      accepted.
    - A server MUST apply a deadline to the handshake and
      SHOULD limit the number of handshakes in progress.
    - An implementation MUST detect a peer that has vanished
      and close the connection, so that its portals and its
      keyboard grab are released.
    - By default a server MUST NOT listen on the network.

  Gateways. A server MAY delegate the network transport to a
  gateway, a separate process that terminates TLS, accepts
  only allowed identities, and relays the stream to the
  server over a Unix socket reserved for it. The gateway
  tells the server who the client is with ProxyIdentity,
  which MUST be the first message on the connection and MUST
  NOT be sent twice.

    - A server MUST accept ProxyIdentity only on the socket
      reserved for gateways, and only from a peer whose uid
      it has been configured to trust.
    - ProxyIdentity on any other connection is a protocol
      violation.
    - A connection on the gateway socket that does not begin
      with ProxyIdentity MUST be closed.

  Authorization. Authentication says who a client is. What it
  may do is a separate decision, and the default is to deny.
  For the keyboard grab, a server MUST check the identity
  against its grab policy: a local client by uid, a network
  client by identity. A network client MUST NOT be granted
  anything because of the uid of a gateway.

  Scope. VPort MUST NOT be used to enter PINs or card data.
  That needs certified hardware.


--[ 9. LIMITS ]-----------------------------------------------

  A client must stay inside these limits. A server disconnects
  a client that goes beyond them.

  Maximum payload size                        1 MiB
  Identity length                      1 to 128 bytes
  Maximum clients                                64
  Maximum portals per client                      8
  Maximum uncommitted commands per portal      4096
  Maximum images per client                      32
  Maximum image memory per client             8 MiB
  Maximum image width or height                4096
  Text length                          1 to 256 bytes
  Text height                          1 to 512 pixels
  Output backlog per client                  64 KiB


--[ 10. ERRORS ]----------------------------------------------

  The server does not send error messages. A client that
  breaks the protocol is disconnected, and the reason is
  written to the server log. Causes include:

      - unknown opcode
      - payload size the opcode does not allow
      - payload larger than the maximum
      - message before Hello, or Hello twice
      - identity message on a local connection, sent twice,
        sent after Hello, or with an invalid identity
      - gateway connection that does not begin with an
        identity
      - unsupported protocol version
      - server-only message sent by a client
      - unknown portal id, or portal id already in use
      - unknown image id, or image id already in use
      - unsupported image format
      - image dimensions out of range
      - image data outside the bounds of the image
      - text that is not valid UTF-8
      - text height out of range
      - any limit in section 9 exceeded


